abstract:
Clustering without committing to k in advance. Agglomerative clustering starts with every point as its own cluster and repeatedly merges the two closest, producing a whole nested family of clusterings drawn as a dendrogram. The number of clusters is chosen afterwards, by cutting the tree. We cover all four linkage criteria and what each one assumes, the Lance–Williams recurrence, how to read k off the merge heights, cophenetic correlation, and the $O(n^2)$ wall.}
